mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — full worked threads\n\nTwo complete threads showing architecture + post craft, in the blunt indie-founder voice from the\nearlier skills (short, dry, no exclamation marks, concrete). Plus a \"should've been one post\"\ncontrast. Your output uses the loaded `voice.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Example 1 — Listicle thread (X) · goal: saves + follows\n\n**Spine:** opener promises 5 lessons from deleting a feature → one lesson per post → close recaps +\nRT mechanic.\n\n**1/ (opener — hook + promise)**\n> We spent 6 months building our most-requested feature. We deleted it this week. Nobody used it.\n> 5 lessons, so you don't burn 6 months too 🧵\n\n**2/**\n> \"Requested\" is not \"used.\"\n> People told us they wanted it in every sales call. Then nobody touched it. Watch what users do,\n> not what they say.\n\n**3/**\n> The boring feature wins.\n> The thing we kept skipping — a dull little export button — shipped in 2 days and is now our most-\n> used feature. Build the boring thing people already do by hand.\n\n**4/**\n> Sales calls are not a roadmap.\n> Prospects describe the feature that would close *them*. Build for the words and you ship things\n> nobody opens.\n\n**5/**\n> Shipping less is a feature.\n> Every feature is forever: docs, bugs, support, UI clutter. Deleting the dead one made the whole\n> product better.\n\n**6/**\n> The meta-lesson: we were building to feel productive, not to be useful.\n> Now we watch behavior first, build second.\n\n**7/ (close + CTA)**\n> That's it — 5 lessons from torching 6 months of work.\n> If this was useful, RT the first post so the next founder sees it 👇\n> (I write about building small software in public — follow for more.)\n\n*Notice: opener hooks + promises and stands alone; one lesson per post; each front-loads its point\nand pulls forward; the close lands and asks for the reshare.*\n\n---\n\n## Example 2 — Story thread (X) · goal: reach + connection\n\n**Spine:** opener drops into near-failure → beats of the 30 days → the turn → the lesson.\n\n**1/**\n> Two years ago we had 6 weeks of runway and no idea why churn was killing us. Here's the 30 days\n> that saved the company 👇\n\n**2/**\n> First we did the thing founders avoid: we called 20 customers who'd left. Terrifying. Also the\n> most useful week of the year.\n\n**3/**\n> The pattern was brutal and obvious. They didn't churn because of price or features. They churned\n> because they never got the first win. Onboarding was the leak.\n\n**4/**\n> So we threw out the feature roadmap for a month and rebuilt one thing: the first 10 minutes.\n\n**5/ (close)**\n> Churn halved in 6 weeks. Same product, different first impression.\n> The lesson we almost died learning: people don't leave bad products, they leave products they\n> never figured out.\n> If you're pre-churn-panic, fix the first 10 minutes.\n\n---\n\n## Contrast — this should've been ONE post\n\n> \"Unpopular opinion: posting daily is overrated. Consistency you can sustain beats volume you\n> can't. 3 great posts a week > 14 mediocre ones.\"\n\nThat's a single sharp take with no sub-beats. As a \"thread\" it'd be padded filler. **Leave it as\none post.** Knowing this is half the skill.\n\n---\n\n## What the examples show\n\n- The **opener hooks + promises** and works alone.\n- **One idea per thread, one beat per post.**\n- Every post **front-loads** and **pulls forward**; no filler.\n- The **close lands the payoff** and makes **one** CTA (the reshare mechanic on X).\n- Some ideas **shouldn't be threads** — and the skill says so.\n"
